reformulations.equivalence_argument:
Every element of the set is greater than 4, so 0 can never be a real index. I proved three facts in a scratch file importing the module, writing S for the set:
(i) `sInf S = 0 ↔ ¬ ∃ n, 4 < n ∧ (a n).Prime`;
(ii) if `sInf S = m` with `m ≠ 0`, then `4 < m`, `(a m).Prime`, and `m ≤ k` for every other k > 4 with `a k` prime;
(iii) if m > 4, `a m` is prime and m is the least such index, then `sInf S = m`.
So the Lean value determines the source's answer ("never", or the index of the next prime), and the answer determines the value.

The bound `4 < n` literally means "after a(4)". It gives the same set as the bound "after a(7)", because a 5, a 6 and a 7 are composite (proved in Lean). It also gives the same set as the bound "after a(19)", given the source's statement that a(8)–a(19) are composite.
